{"findings": [{"file": "pkg/server/module_server_test.go", "line": 35, "severity": "medium", "category": "test_gap", "title": "Postgres skip added — hides eager-init regression", "body": "The PR adds a hard skip for the postgres backend in this storage-server integration test:
  if dbType == "postgres" {
      t.Skip("skipping - test not working with postgres in Drone. Works locally.")
  }
The TODO comment makes it clear this is a new failure caused by this PR. Since the only behavior change in this PR that affects the storage server boot path is the move to eager initialization in NewResourceServer, the skip is most likely papering over a real regression in the eager-init path (e.g. an init that does not tolerate the postgres connection configuration used by this test, or a timeout when the postgres backend initializes). Hiding the failure with t.Skip means the regression is not caught in CI and is left to rot. Investigate the underlying cause (the test sets up postgres with a hardcoded `db_host=localhost`; verify whether NewResourceServer/Init blocks or fails in this configuration) and either fix the init path or fix the test config — do not merge with a skip."}, {"file": "pkg/storage/unified/search/bleve.go", "line": 137, "severity": "medium", "category": "concurrency", "title": "BuildIndex no longer serializes per key — duplicate indexes possible", "body": "The previous version held `b.cacheMu` for the entire BuildIndex call, so concurrent calls for the same key were serialized and the cached index was consistent. The new version only locks when assigning `b.cache[key] = idx`. Two concurrent calls to `BuildIndex` for the same `NamespacedResource` (for example, two simultaneous `Search` requests that both find the cache empty via `getOrCreateIndex`, or the init goroutine racing with an incoming search) will both:
  1. Open separate bleve indexes (file or memory),
  2. Run the builder to write all documents,
  3. Flush,
  4. Race to write `b.cache[key]` — last writer wins, the loser's index is orphaned in memory and the work is wasted.
The PR description frames this as "finer grain locking", but the same-key duplication problem is exactly the TODO called out in `searchSupport.getOrCreateIndex` ("We want to block while building the index and return the same index for the key … simple mutex not great … we don't want to block while anything in building, just the same key"). A per-key lock (e.g. a `sync.Map` of in-progress builds, or a sharded mutex keyed by `NamespacedResource`) is needed to actually fix the duplicate-build race while keeping different-key concurrency."}, {"file": "pkg/storage/unified/resource/server.go", "line": 258, "severity": "medium", "category": "api", "title": "Eager Init inside NewResourceServer changes the constructor's contract", "body": "`NewResourceServer` now calls `s.Init(ctx)` synchronously before returning. The constructor will now block for the full duration of search-index build (potentially many seconds for large installations) and return an error if init fails. Previously the same errors would surface on the first RPC and the constructor returned quickly.
This breaks any caller that expected the constructor to be cheap (tests, callers that wire up dependencies lazily, callers that want to surface a degraded server, callers that explicitly rely on `server.Init` being invoked separately). The PR description itself flags this concern ("Does anything else depend on initializing US lazily? What could this break?") without answering it. Verify all callers of `NewResourceServer` and the SQL `NewBackend` (which now also returns only after `Init` succeeds, since its backend implements the resource LifecycleHooks path) before merging — at minimum, document the contract change on the function and make sure module startup won't now fail outright where it previously degraded."}, {"file": "pkg/storage/unified/resource/search.go", "line": 207, "severity": "medium", "category": "bug", "title": "Event-pump goroutine in search.init panics on channel close", "body": "Inside `searchSupport.init`:
  go func() {
      for {
          v := <-events
          s.handleEvent(watchctx, v)
      }
  }()
The SQL backend closes `events` from `poller` when `b.done` fires (server Stop). After the channel is closed, `v` is a nil `*WrittenEvent`; the very next call to `s.handleEvent` does `evt.Type` and dereferences nil, panicking. The channel-close case must be handled, e.g. `for v := range events` (which terminates cleanly) or `for { v, ok := <-events; if !ok { return } ... }`. With this PR making init synchronous and more central to startup/shutdown, this latent panic on normal server Stop is more likely to be hit."}]}

findings.body:
The PR adds a hard skip for the postgres backend in this storage-server integration test:
  if dbType == "postgres" {
      t.Skip("skipping - test not working with postgres in Drone. Works locally.")
  }
The TODO comment makes it clear this is a new failure caused by this PR. Since the only behavior change in this PR that affects the storage server boot path is the move to eager initialization in NewResourceServer, the skip is most likely papering over a real regression in the eager-init path (e.g. an init that does not tolerate the postgres connection configuration used by this test, or a timeout when the postgres backend initializes). Hiding the failure with t.Skip means the regression is not caught in CI and is left to rot. Investigate the underlying cause (the test sets up postgres with a hardcoded `db_host=localhost`; verify whether NewResourceServer/Init blocks or fails in this configuration) and either fix the init path or fix the test config — do not merge with a skip.

Inside `searchSupport.init`:
  go func() {
      for {
          v := <-events
          s.handleEvent(watchctx, v)
      }
  }()
The SQL backend closes `events` from `poller` when `b.done` fires (server Stop). After the channel is closed, `v` is a nil `*WrittenEvent`; the very next call to `s.handleEvent` does `evt.Type` and dereferences nil, panicking. The channel-close case must be handled, e.g. `for v := range events` (which terminates cleanly) or `for { v, ok := <-events; if !ok { return } ... }`. With this PR making init synchronous and more central to startup/shutdown, this latent panic on normal server Stop is more likely to be hit.
